From: Martin Schreiner <martin.schreiner@suse.com>
Subject: mod_ssl, ab: Ignore SSLRandomSeed / skip own PRNG seeding with OpenSSL 1.1.1+
References: bsc#1282733 bsc#1246913
Upstream: backport of
  https://github.com/apache/httpd/commit/c2321e5b8fa6792662deaaeb05f1c24bd71503eb (r1877467)
  https://github.com/apache/httpd/commit/5be0a5fe0ad85448bf7c4ed3c88cd84ba57534d9 (r1895659)

With OpenSSL 1.1.1 and later, the library seeds its DRBG itself and
handles fork() safety on its own, so there is no need for mod_ssl to
feed it entropy.  "SSLRandomSeed connect builtin" made mod_ssl call
RAND_seed() on every new connection; with OpenSSL 3.5 each such call
reseeds the primary DRBG from jitterentropy, which costs several ms of
CPU per handshake and saturates loaded servers.

With OpenSSL >= 1.1.1, SSLRandomSeed is now accepted but ignored (a
warning is logged), and mod_ssl fails startup if the library PRNG is not
properly seeded.  ab gets the same treatment.

Differences to upstream:
  - pid and aRandSeed are kept in SSLModConfigRec (and aRandSeed is
    still initialised), because that structure is cached in the process
    pool and reused across graceful restarts after mod_ssl.so is
    reloaded, so its layout must not change within the 2.4.x series.
  - CHANGES and docs/log-message-tags hunks dropped.

